What I Learned About Accepting User Input in C

Today, I learned how to accept user input in the C programming language using scanf(), getchar(), and fgets().
I learned that scanf() is used to get different types of input from the user, such as integers (%d), floating-point numbers (%f), and characters (%c).
I also learned how to use variables to store the information entered by the user.
I learned that fgets() is useful for accepting a full name or a sentence because it can read spaces, unlike scanf(). 
I also understood the purpose of getchar(), which helps remove the leftover newline character from the input buffer before using fgets().
Another thing I learned was how to use strlen() from the string.h library to remove the newline character at the end of the name.
Overall, this lesson helped me understand how to take different types of input from users and display the information using printf(). 
It also improved my understanding of variables, data types, and basic input and output operations in C.
